@node Naming system
@section Naming system

In Objective-C, variable names use @code{case-sensitive} naming (conventionally @code{camelCase} for variables and @code{UpperCamelCase} for classes).
All keywords are case-sensitive and written in lowercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: @interface, @implementation, @end, @public, @protected, @private, @package, @classmethod, @selector, @protocol, @class, @selector, @required, @optional, if, else, switch, case, default, for, while, do, break, continue, return, goto, sizeof, typedef, enum, struct, union, extern, static, const, volatile, inline, _Bool, _Complex, _Imaginary, inline, restrict, _Atomic, _Thread_local, _Alignas, _Alignof, _Generic, _Static_assert, _Noreturn
@item Operators: +, -, *, /, %, =, ==, !=, <, >, <=, >=, &&, ||, !, &, |, ^, ~, <<, >>, +=, -=, *=, /=, %=, &=, |=, ^=, <<=, >>=, [], (), ., ->, ++, --, ,, :, ;, {...}
@item String delimiters: Double quotes (@code{"..."}) or wide string literals (@code{L"..."})
@item Comments: Single line starts with @code{//}, block comment @code{/* ... */}
@end table

@node Basic structure
@section Basic structure for a stand-alone routine or program

An Objective-C program consists of class definitions and functions that are executed sequentially unless altered by control flow.

@example
// Initialize game
#import <Foundation/Foundation.h>

@interface GameState : NSObject
@property (assign) int score;
@property (assign) int livesLeft;
@property (assign) int currentRoom;
@end

@implementation GameState
- (instancetype)init {
    self = [super init];
    if (self) {
        _score = 0;
        _livesLeft = 3;
        _currentRoom = 1;
    }
    return self;
}
@end

int main(int argc, const char * argv[]) {
    @autoreleasepool {
        GameState *gameState = [[GameState alloc] init];
        [gameState initializeGame];
        while (!gameState.gameOver) {
            [gameState processInput];
            [gameState updateGame];
            [gameState renderDisplay];
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

@implementation GameState (Methods)
- (void)initializeGame {
    self.score = 0;
    self.livesLeft = 3;
    self.currentRoom = 1;
}

- (void)processInput {
    // Simulate input handling
    if ([[NSUserDefaults standardUserDefaults] boolForKey:@"rightArrowPressed"]) {
        self.currentRoom += 10;
    }
}

- (void)updateGame {
    // Game logic updates
}

- (void)renderDisplay {
    // Rendering code
}
@end
@end example

@node Basic structure object
@section Basic structure for an object method

Objective-C supports object-oriented programming through classes and methods (instance and class methods).

@example
// Define a Player class
@interface Player : NSObject
@property (assign) int x;
@property (assign) int y;
@property (assign) int sprite;
@end

@implementation Player
- (instancetype)initWithX:(int)x y:(int)y sprite:(int)sprite {
    self = [super init];
    if (self) {
        _x = x;
        _y = y;
        _sprite = sprite;
    }
    return self;
}

- (void)movePlayerWithDX:(int)dx dy:(int)dy {
    self.x += dx;
    self.y += dy;
}
@end

// Create instance
Player *player = [[Player alloc] initWithX:100 y:100 sprite:5];
@end example

@node Calling routines
@section Calling a routine (nullary)

To call a function or method that takes no arguments:

@example
initializeGraphics();
clearScreen();
playStartupMusic();
[someObject someMethodWithNoArgs];
@end example

@node Calling routines value
@section Calling a routine with a value (in accumulator)

To call a function that returns a single value:

@example
int score = calculateBonus(level, timeLeft);
double bonusFactor = getInputFactor();
float volume = getMasterVolume();
[someObject someMethodWithReturnValue];
@end example

@node Calling routines giving
@section Calling a routine giving a value (in accumulator)

Not applicable in Objective-C's standard calling convention.

@node Calling routines values
@section Calling a routine with values in and out (in accumulator)

To call a function with parameters:

@example
drawSprite(spriteId, xPos, yPos);
playSound(soundId, volume, loopFlag);
checkCollision(obj1, obj2, resultVar);
[someObject someMethodWithParam1:param1 andParam2:param2];
@end example

@node Calling library
@section Calling a library routine (all 4 styles)

Objective-C accesses library routines through standard function calls or object methods:

@example
loadLevel(levelNumber);          @r{Direct call}
farProc(libraryAddress);         @r{Far call}
result = libraryFunction(input); @r{Library function}
[object methodWithParam:param1 andOtherParam:param2];  @r{Object method call}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: FunctionName(...)
@item Far: AbsoluteAddress(...) (via FFI or external module)
@item Library: LibraryEntry(...) or FunctionName(...)
@item Object: [OBJECT method:...]
@end table

@node Calling far bank
@section Calling a far bank Service (all 4 styles)

Far procedures in Objective-C are called using the foreign function interface (FFI) or external modules:

@example
// Assume we have a C function farLoadLibrary at address 0x8000
extern void farLoadLibrary(uint32_t addr);

// Call far routine
farLoadLibrary(0x8000);   @r{Call routine in bank 1}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Not used for far calls
@item Far: FFI call to absolute address
@item Library: Not applicable
@item Object: Not applicable
@end table

@node Invoking object
@section Invoking a method on an object (all 4 styles)

Object method invocation in Objective-C uses the bracket notation:

@example
[player movePlayerWithDX:5 dy:10];
[scoreDisplay updateScore:score];
BOOL gameOver = [gameState isGameOver];
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Setting object properties via property assignment
@item Far: Not applicable for methods (objects typically in same memory)
@item Library: Method may call library routines internally
@item Object: METHOD invocation on object instance
@end table

@node Assignment
@section Assigning to a variable

Variables are assigned using the assignment operator (=):

@example
int score = 0;                           @r{Explicit assignment}
int livesLeft = 3;                       @r{Implicit assignment}
int counter = counter + 1;               @r{Increment}
livesLeft = livesLeft - 1;               @r{Decrement}
score = score + 10;                      @r{Arithmetic assignment}
NSString *message = @"Hello, World!";    @r{String assignment}
@end example

@node Assignment object
@section Assigning to an object slot

Object slots (properties) are assigned using dot notation or setter methods:

@example
player.x = 160;                          @r{Set object's x coordinate}
player.y = 100;                          @r{Set object's y coordinate}
player.sprite = 5;                       @r{Set sprite number}
[player setVisible:YES];                 @r{Set visibility flag}
@end example

@node Assignment array
@section Assigning to an array member with a subscript

Objective-C uses NSArray and NSNumber for arrays. Elements are assigned using setObject:atIndex: or NSMutableArray:

@example
NSMutableArray *scoreList = [NSMutableArray arrayWithCapacity:10];
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    [scoreList addObject:@0];
}
NSMutableArray *initialsList = [NSMutableArray arrayWithCapacity:10];
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    [initialsList addObject:@""];
}
NSMutableArray *board = [NSMutableArray arrayWithCapacity:8];
for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
    NSMutableArray *row = [NSMutableArray arrayWithCapacity:8];
    for (int j = 0; j < 8; j++) {
        [row addObject:@0];
    }
    [board addObject:row];
}

[scoreList replaceObjectAtIndex:0 withObject:@100];
[initialsList replaceObjectAtIndex:0 withObject:@"ALI"];
[[board objectAtIndex:2] replaceObjectAtIndex:3 withObject:@1];
@end example

@node Arithmetic
@section Basic arithmetic: addition and subtraction

Basic arithmetic uses the + and - operators:

@example
score = score + 10;                    @r{Addition}
livesLeft = livesLeft - 1;             @r{Subtraction}
int total = width + height;            @r{Add two variables}
int finalPrice = price - discount;     @r{Subtraction}
int delta = newValue - oldValue;       @r{Subtraction}
@end example

@node Arithmetic bit
@section Basic arithmetic: bit-shifting for mutiplication or division

Bit-shifting for multiplication/division by powers of two uses the << and >> operators:

@example
int result = value << 2;               @r{Left shift 2 bits (multiply by 4)}
int result = value >> 1;               @r{Right shift 1 bit (divide by 2)}
int result = value * 4;                @r{Multiply by 4}
int result = value / 2;                @r{Divide by 2}
int result = value * 8;                @r{Multiply by 8}
int result = value / 16;               @r{Divide by 16}
@end example

@node Complex arithmetic
@section Complex arithmetic: multiple expressions

Complex arithmetic expressions follow standard precedence rules:

@example
int total = (baseSalary * 12) + bonus - deductions;
double average = (sum1 + sum2 + sum3) / 3.0;
double distance = sqrt((x2 - x1)*(x2 - x1) + (y2 - y1)*(y2 - y1));
@end example

@node Boolean
@section Boolean expressions: equality and inequalities

Boolean expressions use logical operators and comparison operators:

@example
if (score > 1000) { ... }             @r{Greater than}
if (score < 50) { ... }               @r{Less than}
if (score == 100) { ... }             @r{Equality}
if (score != 0) { ... }               @r{Inequality}
if (x >= 0 && x < MAX_X) { ... }      @r{Combined conditions}
if (button == BUTTON_A || button == BUTTON_B) { ... } @r{Alternative conditions}
@end example

@node If then else
@section Using if/then/else structures and examine/cond structures

Conditional execution uses if/else/switch statements:

@example
if (temperature > THOT) {
    status = @"HOT";
} else if (temperature > TCOLD) {
    status = @"WARM";
} else {
    status = @"COLD";
}

switch (answer) {
    case 'Y':
    case 'y':
        response = @"YES";
        break;
    case 'N':
    case 'n':
        response = @"NO";
        break;
    default:
        response = @"INVALID";
        break;
}
@end example

@node Performing
@section Performing loops

Loops are implemented using for, while, and do-while loops:

@example
// for loop equivalent (known iterations)
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    NSLog(@"Count: %d", i);
}

// while loop equivalent (pre-test)
int i = 1;
while (i <= 10) {
    NSLog(@"Count: %d", i);
    i++;
}

// do-while loop equivalent (post-test)
do {
    NSLog(@"Continue? (Y/N): ");
    scanf(" %c", &response);
} while (toupper(response) == 'Y');

// fast enumeration for collections
for (id obj in someArray) {
    NSLog(@"%@", obj);
}
@end example

@node Breaking loops
@section Breaking out of loops safely

Early loop exit is achieved using break statement:

@example
// Exit for loop early
for (int i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {
    if (array[i] == TARGET_VALUE) {
        NSLog(@"Found at index: %d", i);
        break;                    @r{Exit loop immediately}
    }
}

// Skip current iteration in while loop
while (condition) {
    if (skipCondition) {
        // Do nothing, effectively skipping
        continue;
    }
    // Process
}

// Nested loop control
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= 10; j++) {
        if (matrix[i][j] == 0) {
            break;                @r{Exit inner loop only}
        }
        // Process non-zero elements
    }
}
@end example

@node Dialogue
@section Calling a dialogue script (Forth scripts)

Objective-C does not directly call Forth dialogue scripts. Instead, it uses
its own methods. However, through the semantic-sugar
layer, Objective-C commands can be mapped to calls of the shared Fountain/Forth
runtime library:

@example
// Original Objective-C code
if ((x > 100) && (x < 200)) {
    [someObject showMessage:MSG_WELCOME];
}

// Desugared via semantic-sugar layer
if ((x > 100) && (x < 200)) {
    (:call-acc :target "ShowWelcomeMessage" :using nil)
}
@end example

@node Tiles
@section Manipulating tiles

Tile manipulation is performed through direct video memory access
or through library routines for tiled backgrounds:

@example
// Direct memory access (mode-dependent)
// Assuming direct memory access via pointer
uint8_t *videoMemory = (uint8_t *)0xB8000;
int offset = (row * 80 + col) * 2;
videoMemory[offset] = tileNum;
videoMemory[offset + 1] = attribute;

// Library routine approach
loadTileSet(tileSetId);
placeTile(layer, x, y, tileIndex);
scrollBackground(dx, dy);
@end example

@node Decals
@section Manipulating decals

Decals (sprites or movable objects) are manipulated using:

@example
// Hardware sprite approach (if available)
initSprites();
setSpritePosition(spriteId, x, y);
setSpriteImage(spriteId, imageId);
showSprite(spriteId, -1);   @r{-1 = visible}

// Software sprite approach
typedef struct {
    int x;
    int y;
    int frame;
} Sprite;

Sprite sprite = {100, 50, 0};

sprite.x = 100;
sprite.y = 50;
sprite.frame = (sprite.frame + 1) % 4;
@end example

@node Objects
@section Manipulating objects

Object manipulation depends on the implementation approach:

@example
// Using Objective-C classes
@interface GameObject : NSObject
@property (assign) int x;
@property (assign) int y;
@property (assign) int width;
@property (assign) int height;
@property (assign) BOOL active;
@end

@implementation GameObject
- (instancetype)initWithX:(int)x y:(int)y width:(int)width height:(int)height {
    self = [super init];
    if (self) {
        _x = x;
        _y = y;
        _width = width;
        _height = height;
        _active = YES;
    }
    return self;
}

- (void)moveWithDX:(int)dx dy:(int)dy {
    self.x += dx;
    self.y += dy;
}
@end

// Create instances
NSMutableArray *gameObjects = [NSMutableArray arrayWithCapacity:20];
for (int i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
    GameObject *obj = [[GameObject alloc] initWithX:100 y:100 width:16 height:16];
    [gameObjects addObject:obj];
}

// Update object position
for (GameObject *obj in gameObjects) {
    [obj moveWithDX:dx dy:dy];
}

// Check active status and draw
for (GameObject *obj in gameObjects) {
    if (obj.active) {
        [self drawObject:obj];
    }
}
@end example

@node Unsupported
@section Unsupported language features

The following Objective-C features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O operations (fopen, fread, fwrite, fclose)
@item Graphics commands (Core Graphics, OpenGL ES)
@item Hardware-specific features (Core Motion, Core Location)
@item Extended memory handling (unless emulated)
@item Multithreading or concurrent execution (unless using GCD or NSThread)
@item Exception handling (@try, @catch, @finally, @throw)
@item Pointer arithmetic and manual memory management
@item Preprocessor directives (#define, #ifdef) beyond basic macros
@item Automatic Reference Counting (ARC) vs manual retain/release (unless specified)
@item Blocks and Grand Central Dispatch (unless using external library)
@end table

